# requirements.in -- the human-edited SOURCE of dependency floors. Never
# install directly from this file. `requirements.txt` is the pip-compile
# generated, fully pinned lock file actually installed everywhere in this
# repo (seed_lab.sh, Dockerfile, ai-release.yml, azure.yaml) -- regenerate
# it after editing this file with:
#   pip install pip-tools --break-system-packages
#   python3.12 -m piptools compile requirements.in -o requirements.txt
# Use Python 3.12 specifically -- pip-compile writes the interpreter's
# own major.minor version into requirements.txt's autogenerated header
# comment, and ai-release.yml's `deterministic-tests` job diffs the
# regenerated file byte-for-byte against the committed one (not just the
# dependency list) on the same Python 3.12 `setup-python` step it uses
# everywhere else in that workflow. Regenerating with any other locally
# installed Python (3.10/3.11/3.13, whatever `python3`/`pip-compile`
# resolve to on your machine) reliably fails that check on the header
# line alone, even when every resolved package version is identical.
# ai-release.yml's `deterministic-tests` job re-runs this and fails the
# build if requirements.txt drifts from what this file would produce --
# see roadmap Section 4.1, "Dependency pinning."

